            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "After all, there seems to be a bug, just a different one :) Minimal snippet:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nconfig = function ()\r\n    local sessions = require('mini.sessions')\r\n    local sessions_dir = vim.fn.stdpath('state') .. \"/sessions/\"\r\n\r\n    sessions.setup({\r\n        directory = sessions_dir,\r\n        file = 'autosession',\r\n    })\r\n\r\n    vim.keymap.set(\r\n        'n',\r\n        '<leader>sw',\r\n        function() sessions.write(sessions_dir .. 'autosession', { verbose = true }) end\r\n    )\r\nend\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis returns:\r\n\r\n```\r\nError executing vim.schedule lua callback: vim/_editor.lua:0: nvim_exec2():\r\nVim(mksession):E190: Cannot open \r\n\"/home/user/.local/state/nvim/sessions/home/user/.local/state/nvim/sessions/autosession\"\r\nfor writing\r\n```\r\n\r\nLooks like `write()` ignores first argument being an absolute path while passing simple `'autosession'` results in the session file at `$PWD`.\r\n\r\nThis happens irrespectively of using either `vim.fn.expand('$XDG_STATE_HOME')` or `vim.fn.stdpath('state')`.\r\n\r\nPassing an absolute path as the string yields the same duplication.\r\n\r\n@echasnovski , would you like this referenced as the new issue?",
