            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "> After all, there seems to be a bug, just a different one :)\r\n\r\nThis is still not a bug. `write()` [accepts first argument as session **name**](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/f54410c7f866f51711118fd82274043cf731b63d/doc/mini-sessions.txt#L162-L163) not a full path. The directory where it is written is [determined based on the name](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/f54410c7f866f51711118fd82274043cf731b63d/doc/mini-sessions.txt#L153-L158).\r\n\r\nThe `config.file` option is specifically intended to be the [name for local sessions](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/f54410c7f866f51711118fd82274043cf731b63d/doc/mini-sessions.txt#L86), meaning the ones which create session file in the current working directory. This (along with `autoread = true`) allows the workflow of automatically restoring session which was in the current directory.\r\n\r\n------\r\n\r\nIf I understand correctly, the behavior that worked just fine is a \"have single session be read during startup and written before closing Neovim\". 'mini.sessions' is not specifically designed for this kind of workflow, but it can be replicated with the following setup:\r\n\r\n```lua\r\nfunction()\r\n  require('mini.sessions').setup({\r\n    autoread = true,\r\n    directory = vim.fn.stdpath('state') .. '/sessions/',\r\n  })\r\nend\r\n```\r\n\r\nOn first run there will be an error about no detected sessions (because there aren't any). Follow it with `:lua MiniSessions.write('autosession')` which should write a session file to `config.directory`. After that it should work as a single global session: it will be loaded during startup because it is the latest used session and written before closing Neovim because it is the current session.",
